# The Unicode Character Database file the corpus's `ucd:` provenance names.
#
# `ucd:17.0.0/PropList.txt` is this file. The number area's whitespace set (StrWhiteSpaceChar) takes
# its Space_Separator members from the White_Space lines whose general category is Zs. It is the file
# Broiler.VM pins (Broiler.VM/src/tests/unicode/pins/unicode.pin, rule N22), named here by the same
# digest. The generator re-derives the set from it when given --proplist and refuses a file whose
# digest differs.
#
# 2026-10-16, corpus revision 18 (milestone SJL-7.3, the Unicode area). The pin now names every file
# scripts/conformance/generate_unicode_vectors.py and its reference computation, unicoderef.py
# (`ref:unicoderef@1`), read: the UCD 17.0.0 files below, the auxiliary break data and the three break
# tests, NormalizationTest.txt, the two emoji 17.0 sequence files, and ECMA-262's three property
# tables. Each is named by the same length and SHA-256 as Broiler.Unicode's pin,
# Broiler.Unicode/data/unicode/pins/unicode.pin, at Broiler.Unicode efbdc1d, itself a copy of Broiler.VM's
# pin, which Broiler.VM's rule I4 holds equal to it; nothing was retrieved again.
#
# The corpus archives none of these files. The generator takes the directory that holds them,
# `--ucd <dir>`, normally Broiler.Unicode's data/unicode/pins/, and refuses every file whose length or
# SHA-256 is not the one recorded here, before a byte of it is used.
#
# WHAT A STANZA MEANS. `file <name>` opens a stanza for a Unicode data file, named as `ucd:17.0.0/<name>`
# cites it; `spec-table <name>` opens one for an ECMA-262 property table, a file of the tc39/ecma262
# revision sources/ecma262.pin names (its spec.html imports it into the clause UnicodeMatchProperty),
# which unicoderef.py reads for the property names and aliases ECMA-262 admits and which no `ucd:`
# entry names. `path` is where the file sits under the --ucd directory, as Broiler.Unicode's archive lays
# it out; `bytes` and `sha256` are its length and digest. The PropList.txt stanza, first, is revision 1's,
# with its `path` line added at revision 18. The two emoji sequence files are Unicode 17.0.0's emoji data
# (https://www.unicode.org/Public/17.0.0/emoji/), which Broiler.VM's pin and Broiler.Unicode's hold
# beside the UCD; `ucd:17.0.0/emoji-sequences.txt` and `ucd:17.0.0/emoji-zwj-sequences.txt` cite them.
# DerivedGeneralCategory.txt is read only by unicoderef.py's own tests, which check UnicodeData.txt's
# General_Category against it.

